    "base_commit": "12298a851539c338845fc301816fe21500418bc2",
    "children": [
      {
        "objective": "In src/nbody_kernel.cu, retain one thread per target and the existing force arithmetic while cooperatively staging source x, y, z, and mass tiles in shared memory for reuse across the CTA’s targets. Handle partial source tiles and target tails safely, retain self-interaction exclusion, and keep shared memory within the 49,152-byte limit. Sweep feasible tile sizes and matching block/items_per_block settings; use only the permitted source and launch files. Judge candidates by full correctness and the official per-size geometric-mean timing protocol.",
        "role": "T1",
        "subject": "CTA-level shared-memory reuse of source-particle fields"
      },
      {
        "objective": "Explore a warp-cooperative traversal in which each warp owns one target and its lanes accumulate disjoint source-particle subsets, then reduce the three force components within the warp; one lane writes the complete output state. For this mapping, make items_per_block equal the number of target warps per CTA (block/32), and handle N tails, self pairs, coincident positions, and all output fields correctly. Keep this path distinct from shared-memory tiling and measure only candidates passing all correctness checks.",
        "role": "T2",
        "subject": "Warp-parallel inner-loop reduction per target"
      },
      {
        "objective": "Keep the one-thread-per-target traversal, force equations, and memory strategy unchanged; isolate launch-geometry effects by comparing legal block sizes with items_per_block equal to block and shared_memory_bytes zero. In particular, test whether smaller CTAs improve the measured workload without assuming that more CTAs guarantee a gain. Preserve the exact JSON key set and validate every supported size before comparing official timings.",
        "role": "T3",
        "subject": "Launch-geometry tuning for the baseline ownership mapping"
      },
      {
        "objective": "Keep the one-thread-per-target and source traversal structure while exploring algebraically equivalent interaction arithmetic, such as explicit rsqrtf-based inverse-distance-cubed scaling and cancellation of the target-mass multiply/divide. Preserve float32 softening, self-pair handling, velocity-then-position update order, and exact mass copying. Do not enable global fast math; validate numerical tolerances, nonfinite-output checks, and the extreme-mass case before judging timing.",
        "role": "T4",
        "subject": "Per-interaction arithmetic throughput with correctness-bounded transforms"
      }
    ],
